doctype html
html(lang="en")
    include includes/head.pug
    +seo("About the Library", "How The Library was brought about: Borges, a GPL engine, and a personal rebuild.")

    body
        include includes/nav.pug

        main.BodyStyle
            img.CharcoalArt.CharcoalArt--banner(src="/image/art/about-hexagon.jpg", alt="Charcoal stencil of a hexagonal gallery of shelves")

            h2 How this was brought about
            p.initial
                span Borges
                |
                | imagined a library that already contained every book that could ever be written. Not a metaphor stored on a shelf — a total space, hexagonal rooms without end, volumes whose pages are noise until a sentence you recognize surfaces by chance.
            p In 1941 that idea lived only as a story. Decades later, people began building working versions of it: engines that
                |
                |
                em generate
                |
                | books from mathematics instead of storing them. Tom Snelling’s
                |
                |
                a(href="https://github.com/tdjsnelling/babel", target="_blank", rel="noopener noreferrer") babel
                |
                | (GPL-3.0) is one of the rare implementations that models every unique
                |
                em book
                | , not merely every unique page — so a thought can continue across a spine.
            p This site,
                |
                |
                strong The Library
                | , is a personal fork of that work. The bijection stays the same: the same coordinates still open the same volumes. What changes is the doorway — darker chrome, quieter copy, tools meant for wandering rather than explaining yourself to a SaaS dashboard.
            p Nothing here claims the generated text is meaningful because it was found. Existence is cheap in an infinite catalog. Wonder is not.

            h2 What the Library is
            p Every book has 410 pages of 40 lines of 80 characters — 1,312,000 symbols drawn from a 32-character alphabet. The number of such books is
                |
                |
                span.nobreak 32
                    span.super 1,312,000
                | . They are not downloaded. They are computed from an index by a reversible map. The same index always yields the same book.
            p You search by constructing a book that contains your text, then reading the coordinate the math assigns to it. You explore by walking room, wall, shelf, book, and page.

            h2 How the math works
            ol
                li Room, wall, shelf, and book become a sequential book index.
                li That index is multiplied modulo a fixed constant to produce a 1,312,000-digit base-32 value.
                li Digits become letters. The inverse recovers the index from text.
            p Addresses look like
                |
                |
                span.code room.wall.shelf.book.page
                |
                | — for example
                |
                span.code 1.2.3.4.5
                | . Long rooms are shortened in URLs with a hash bookmark.
            p Deeper notes:
                |
                |
                span.code MATHEMATICS.md
                |
                | in
                |
                a(href="https://github.com/ashlessscythe/babel", target="_blank", rel="noopener noreferrer") the source repository
                | .

            h2 Is it fake?
            p No. Pages are not written to disk after you view them. Storing the whole Library is impossible; computing a page from its index is not.

            h2 Prior art
            p Borges’ story is the root.
                |
                a(href="https://libraryofbabel.info", target="_blank", rel="noopener noreferrer") libraryofbabel.info
                |
                | is another exploration — page-oriented, with rich theory. This fork inherits its completeness claim from Snelling’s book-level engine and remains GPL-bound to that lineage.

            h2 Privacy
            p The Library does not need to know who you are. No accounts. When personal bookmarks arrive, they stay in your browser.

        include includes/footer.pug
